/*Q25. You are developing a simple Student Details System to demonstrate object creation, instance variables, and method invocation in Java.
Create a class named Student with the following instance variables:
• id (int): stores the student's ID.
• name (String): stores the student's name.
Implement the following method in the Student class:
• display(): Displays the student's ID and name in the format ID: <student_id>, Name: <student_name>.
The StudentDetails class should perform the following operations:
1. Read the student ID and name.
2. Create a Student object named s1 using the no-argument constructor.
3. Assign the input values to the object's id and name instance variables.
4. Call display() to display the student details.
Input Format:
• The first line contains an integer id, representing the student id.
• The second line contains a string name, representing the student name.
Output Format:
• Display the student's details in the following format:
ID: <student_id>, Name: <student_name>
Sample Input:
101
John
Sample Output:
ID: 101, Name: John
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;
 
    void display() {
        System.out.println("ID: " + id + ", Name: " + name);
    }
}
 
public class StudentDetails {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int id = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
        String name = sc.nextLine().trim();
        Student s1 = new Student();
        s1.id = id;
        s1.name = name;
        s1.display();
        sc.close();
    }
}
